PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E. Not possible to determine based on the information provided}

The provided facts show that Dilp2 from IPCs reaches NSCs via both hemolymph secretion and retrograde transport to DRNs, and that both routes can be experimentally perturbed. However, every loss-of-function manipulation (fat-body Imp-L2, glial Imp-L2, DRN silencing) simultaneously affects Dilp2 levels or availability in both compartments and/or alters IPC expression itself. The exogenous-insulin rescue likewise bypasses endogenous sources. Because no experiment cleanly isolates one route while leaving the other intact, the data do not allow us to conclude whether hemolymph Dilp2, retrogradely transported Dilp2, both, or neither is the physiologically relevant ligand for NSC reactivation.

(The parenthetical remark that “DRNs may have function independent of Dilp2 absorption” further underscores that the DRN-inhibition result cannot be unambiguously attributed to loss of retrogradely delivered Dilp2.)

